      "context_count": 4,
      "answer": "Paging is a memory management scheme that allows virtual memory to be addressed as if it were main memory, thereby eliminating the need for contiguous allocation of physical memory. This technique involves dividing the virtual address space into fixed-size segments called pages and mapping them onto the corresponding blocks in secondary storage (such as disk). When a program attempts to access a page that is not currently present in RAM, this results in a \"page fault,\" which triggers the system to read the required data from the disk into memory. This process ensures efficient use of both main and secondary memory resources by allowing programs to run without being constrained by physical limitations on contiguous memory allocation.",
